course: dictionary
lesson: 4434
title: Handicap: a disadvantage that can make things fair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Handicap has several meanings, and the context tells you which one. The usual meaning is a disadvantage, something that makes it harder to do well. «Being small was a handicap in basketball» means that being small made the game harder. Test it: swap in disadvantage and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In sport, a handicap can make a contest fair. «A real handicap, like playing with the weaker foot or a head start, makes the game fair and the win real.» The clues are fair and win: the handicap gives the weaker player a chance, so the result means more.
explain: As a verb, to handicap someone means to make it harder for them to do something. Be careful with one older meaning. Calling people «handicapped» is old-fashioned and many find it hurtful, so say «disabled person» or «person with a disability» instead.
fact: Handicap can be a noun (a handicap) or a verb (to handicap someone).
fact: «Handicap» means a disadvantage, something that makes it harder to do well.
fact: In golf and horse racing, a handicap is a score or weight that makes the contest fair; in golf, the lower the handicap, the better the player.
fact: Context clues tell you which meaning of handicap is being used, so read the sentence around it.
fact: «Handicapped» is old-fashioned and many find it hurtful; say «disabled person» or «person with a disability».
exercise: «Being small was a handicap in basketball.» What does handicap mean here? | *A disadvantage that makes it harder to do well | A kind of basketball | A long, fast race | A prize for the smallest player
exercise: In golf, the lower a player's handicap, the | Louder | Wetter | Slower | *Better
exercise: Why does a head start count as a handicap in a race? | *It helps weaker runners compete fairly | It is a kind of medal | It stops the strongest runner from winning | It makes the race longer
test: To handicap someone means to | *Make it harder for them to do something | Help them win easily | Let them sleep in | Give them a prize
test: Which is the kinder, modern way to speak? | *a person with a disability | a handicap case | a handicapped person | a handicap person
exam: Gwil plays left-footed and gives Wynne a head start. What is the purpose of this handicap? | To stop Wynne from playing | To make the game shorter | *To make the game fair between them | To make Gwil win more easily
